+# Design
+## Wire contract
+PATCH /admin/drafts/:id adds optional expectedUpdatedAt (ISO). CLI update reads info to supply it unless --if-unmodified-since provided.
+POST /admin/drafts/:id/revise body: data (merge patch), expectedUpdatedAt (required ISO), idempotencyKey (required opaque 1..128), targetEntityId (optional ULID for legacy ambiguity).
+CLI: content draft revise --id ID --data JSON|@FILE|- --request-id KEY [--if-unmodified-since ISO] [--target-entity-id ID].
+Revision source version defaults to info. Replay checked before freshness, stable request ID required.
+Server mutation result: {draft: IngestDraftDetail, derivedFrom?: string, affectsPublishedContent: false, reused?: boolean}.
+CLI adds environment, sourceDraftId, draftId and status.
+
+## Storage and acceptance
+Persist trusted target/baseline/patch metadata separately from caller source/data. Record new accepted draft -> entity associations. Resolve legacy target only unambiguously; explicit target must be validated.
+Lock target, compare baseline, update original locale in accept transaction. Do not reuse full-replacement CRUD deleting other locales.
+Apply intended changes against current formal baseline, avoiding unrelated stale-data overwrite. Identity/operational fields stay protected.
+Revision descendants preserve trusted metadata or reject unsafe derivation explicitly.
+Showcase has a separate narrow editor; no media clearing, agent dispatch or current-schema acceptance check.
+
+## Rollout
+Migration/API before CLI. PR review only; no production deploy. Staging end-to-end proof follows deployment.
+
+## Tradeoffs and compatibility
+- A distinct revise command makes accepted-to-pending intent explicit; update never silently overwrites accepted content.
+- Caller-owned request IDs survive timeout/restart. Server replay lookup precedes source freshness checks; its fingerprint contains data and targetEntityId, deliberately excluding expectedUpdatedAt. The timestamp protects new operations, while a replay returns the prior result even if the source version changed. Different patch/target with the same key conflicts.
+- The CLI keeps a small local nonblank/length request-ID check for immediate usage errors; the SDK/API remains authoritative and validates all HTTP clients independently.
+- CLI auto-fetch only guards concurrent changes after that read. --if-unmodified-since is required to protect a version reviewed earlier by a human.
+- Old APIs may reject expectedUpdatedAt or lack revise. If a partially upgraded API commits a write but returns a receipt without affectsPublishedContent=false, the new CLI exits 1 with readback guidance; it never retries without the version guard. Deploy API first.
+- Trusted revision metadata is separate from caller-owned source/data to prevent forged target bindings. Additional tables are additive; existing drafts need no destructive backfill. Legacy resolution is deliberately conservative.
+- Narrow per-locale writes preserve other translations; full CRUD replacement and ordinary create-only accept were rejected because they can delete locales or duplicate content.
+
+## Rollback shape
+CLI can revert independently, retaining all content. Once revision rows exist, old create-only API accept is unsafe even if no revision has yet been accepted: disable mutation or roll forward; retain receipts/mappings. After acceptance, corrections require a new reviewed revision, not destructive history rollback. See implement.md and the sites rollout record for release gates.
