+# CLI validation
+
+Validated on 2026-11-20 in WSL Ubuntu with Rust 1.91, target `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`.
+
+`scripts/check.sh` passed: installer encoding, fmt, clippy with `-D warnings`, and all 230 tests.
+Counts: 100 unit, 19 auth flow, 21 CLI binary, 51 content HTTP, 4 credentials,
+24 self-update, 11 update-hint tests. Windows-only tests do not execute on Linux.
+
+Command environment:
+
+```sh
+setpriv --bounding-set=-dac_override,-dac_read_search \
+  env PATH=/usr/lib/rust-1.91/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin \
+  CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/hiapi-issue17-target RUST_TEST_THREADS=1 \
+  bash scripts/check.sh
+```
+
+The WSL default user is root. Dropping DAC override capabilities makes existing read-only-directory
+tests exercise real permission denial. Serial test execution avoids an intermittent Linux ETXTBSY
+in the existing self-updater executable probe; the concurrency test still runs its own concurrent
+operations. Its wrong-platform fixture now chooses a target different from the actual host target
+(the previous hardcoded Linux value matched the WSL host and invalidated the test premise).
+
+After the final help-only wording change, fmt and the binary help regression passed again.
+`git diff --check` passed. No service deployment or staging writes were performed by the CLI implementation.
+
+New regressions cover version-fetch failure, explicit reviewed versions, no retry on 409,
+stable revision requests/replay receipts, JSON context preservation, file/stdin patches,
+5xx readback hints, and malformed mutation receipts.
+
+A real cross-repository local integration also passed: built Rust CLI -> Elysia HTTP -> fresh
+PostgreSQL. It covers Showcase patch/readback/protected-field atomic rejection and accepted FAQ
+revise/replay/accept, preserving the original accepted record, formal entity ID, publication time
+and other locale. No agent jobs were created. Staff introspection used project test fixtures.
+The database replayed all 73 migrations. This does not substitute for deployed staging validation.
